hey everyone it's sarah with registered  nurse RN comm and in this video i'm  going to show you how to load a Lancet  device as a nursing student you will  need to know how to load a Lancet device  especially whenever you're teaching  diabetics how to check their blood sugar  and plus in the hospital setting you may  have to load a Lancet to buy a lot of  hospitals though have their own little  prefilled Lancet devices where you don't  actually have to put it together but  it's always good to know how to do this  now if you are newly diabetic or you're  just wanting to check your blood sugar  I'm going to this video help you out too  because I'm going to show you how to do  this so let's get started okay you're  going to need your Lancet device and  your landset before we get started I'm  going to go a little bit over these  little lancets because there are  different sizes you can get to use when  checking your glucose I'm generally  thirty which is what this lancehead is  is good enough to get a good prick on  your finger but they also have sizes  like 27 gauge 28 gauge 32 gauge and 33  gauge all this is is that it's a number  that represents the size of the needle  now the smaller the number the larger  the needle so a 27 gauge is a larger  needle than a 30 gauge so just pick  accordingly you have really thick skin  you probably need a bigger gauge needle  which would be a smaller number so  that's a little bit about lancets  now let's load our device all devices  are typically the same and they work the  same and what you do is they have an  area where you can twist the cap off and  expose the area where this little  landset is going to go now this area  right here notice when I push it in I  can hit this button right here and it's  going to engage disengage the needle and  whenever I do that that means it's going  to poke your finger so that's just how  that works it's usually always good to  see how the mechanism behind something  works because it helps you understand it  better so now let's put our Lancet in  the device and it has little grooves  that's going to line up with this groove  right here I don't know if you can see  it there might be a glare but there's a  groove right here and it's going to fit  in  side of where the land set is and it  just slides right down in there and what  we're going to do is we're going to go  ahead and engage the needle because you  don't want to engage it while you've  exposed your needle because you're going  to increase your chances of  contaminating the needle and getting  poked yourself so go ahead and just poke  it in and we're going to twist this cap  off to expose the needle and needles  right there it's very very very small  and we're going to gently put the cap  back on and always remember to engage  your needle whenever you put the cap  back on and it's ready to use but  there's still one more step we have to  do now on your device whenever you're  first using it you're going to have to  pick a setting and there's these little  numbers right here it goes from 0 to 5  some devices go up til 10 but all this  is is telling us how deep this needle is  going to go into our finger now here  look in your user guide for your land  set because it'll tell you the different  depths but generally this is how it  works 1 to 2 is for people who have  really thin skin if you're taking  corticosteroids or your hands bruise  easy your fingers bruise is easy you  just have thin skin you probably want to  use a 1 to 2 setting if you have like  average skin like most people you're  young 3 to 4 is probably the best but 5  or higher if you are like a man or woman  who work a lot with your hands you have  very callous fingers you probably want  to use a deeper setting because if you  use a 3 or 4 one or two it's probably  barely I'm going to graze the skin  you're not going to get a good prick so  always adjust your settings ok so that  is how you load your Lancet device now  be sure to check o